===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[differential gear]] in an [[automobile]], etc.
# A [[qualitative]] or [[quantitative]] difference between [[similar]] or [[comparable]] things.
# One of two [[coil]]s of [[conduct]]ing [[wire]] so related to one another or to a [[magnet]] or [[armature]] common to both, that one coil produces [[polar]] action contrary to that of the other.
# A form of [[conductor]] used for dividing and distributing the [[current]] to a series of electric lamps so as to maintain equal action in all.<ref>{{R:Knight AMD}}</ref>
# {{lb|en|calculus}} A quantity representing an [[infinitesimal]] change in a [[variable]], now only used as a [[heuristic]] aid except in [[nonstandard analysis]] but considered rigorous until the 20th century; a [[fluxion]] in {{w|Method of Fluxions|Newtonian calculus}}, now usually written in {{w|Leibniz's notation}} as <math>\operatorname{d}\!x</math>.
# {{lb|en|calculus|of a [[univariate]] [[differentiable]] [[function]] <math>f(x)</math>}} A function giving the change in the [[linear]] [[approximation]] of <math>f</math> at a point <math>x</math> over a small interval <math>\Delta x</math> or <math>\operatorname{d}\!x</math>, the function being called the '''differential of''' <math>f</math> and denoted <math>\operatorname{d}\!f(x,\Delta x)</math>, <math>\operatorname{d}\!f(x)</math>, or simply <math>\operatorname{d}\!f</math>.
#: {{ux|en|If <math>f(x) = x^2</math>, the differential of <math>f</math> is the function <math>\operatorname{d}\!f(x, \Delta x) = f'(x)\Delta x = 2x\Delta x</math>.}}
## Any of several [[generalize|generalizations]] of this concept to functions of several variables or to higher [[order]]s: the [[partial differential]], [[total differential]], {{w|Gateaux differential}}, [[etc.]]
# {{lb|en|{{w|multivariable calculus}}}} The [[Jacobian]] [[matrix]] of a function of several variables.
# {{lb|en|differential geometry|of a [[smooth]] [[map]] <math>\phi</math> between [[smooth manifold]]s}} The [[pushforward]] or [[total derivative]] of <math>\phi</math>: a linear map from the [[tangent space]] at a point <math>x</math> in  <math>\phi</math>'s domain to the tangent space at <math>\phi(x)</math> which is, in a technical sense, the best linear approximation of  <math>\phi</math> at <math>x</math>; denoted <math>\operatorname{d}\!\phi_x</math>.
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} Any of several [[generalize|generalizations]] of the concept(s) above: e.g. the {{w|Kähler differential}} in the setting of [[scheme]]s, the {{w|quadratic differential}} in the theory of [[Riemann surfaces]], etc.

